# width: 120
# The keyboard path: `view.focus_dock` (vim `Ctrl-W D`) puts the keys in
# the strip, `h` / `l` walk a bottom dock, Enter runs the item and hands
# the keys back, Esc leaves without running anything. The run starts on
# Browser — the strip's first item; the `+` ends it (dock-polish).
write a.txt "alpha\n"
open a.txt
ex dock always
expect screen contains "New terminal"
command view.focus_dock
# The cursor starts on the first item; Esc leaves it alone.
key esc
expect screen contains "New terminal"
expect screen lacks "Terminal ("
# The strip holds the keyboard only while the hand is on it: a press
# anywhere else hands the keys back, so an `h` typed into the editor
# afterwards reaches the buffer instead of walking a dock nobody is
# looking at.
command view.focus_dock
click 60 2
type "hl"
key ctrl+s
expect file a.txt contains "hl"
# The cursor starts on Browser, which leads the strip: four `l` steps
# walk it past Claude Code, Codex and HTTP onto New terminal
# (// changed (railmove): the four first-party surfaces are on the
# strip), and Enter opens the shell. Three or fewer would start a real
# `claude` / `codex`; five would land on the `+`, which ends the run —
# the count is load-bearing.
command view.focus_dock
key l
key l
key l
key l
key enter
wait 900
expect pane Terminal
expect screen contains "Terminal ("
